From 7c455868d4d8bce09ef5a0f4dca5ad98860f2398 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: forge-shepherd <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 11:28:42 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(status): read the kernel for the one-glance snapshot, not
 the retired Beads store
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`forge status` built its snapshot from readBeadsSnapshot (.beads/issues.jsonl,
the retired store), so on a kernel-default repo it showed "Ready: none" and the
dead-end "/plan" fallback while the Kernel held a full backlog (bug 40f35797).

- lib/status/snapshot.js: readStatusSnapshot resolves the active issue backend
  (Kernel by default; Beads only when explicitly selected) and reads the Kernel
  via the authoritative ready/blocked/stale/list ops, mapping them into the same
  bucket shape readBeadsSnapshot produced so the presenter consumes either backend
  identically. Resilient by contract: a failed read degrades to an empty bucket.
- lib/commands/status.js: zero-arg status now awaits readStatusSnapshot.
- test/status-command.test.js: pin createTempBeadsRepo to the Beads backend via
  .forge/config.yaml (it seeds .beads) so those fixtures keep exercising the Beads
  snapshot path; the kernel-default path is covered by the new test below.
- test/status/snapshot.test.js: TDD regression coverage — a real seeded kernel
  with ready issues -> snapshot.ready populated -> "Ready: N" + claim fallback
  (never the empty-state /plan dead end); plus readStatusSnapshot backend routing.
- Regenerated the D20 bd call-site kill-list artifact (status.js line shift only;
  counts/tokens unchanged) so `release check` stays at 0 blockers.

Before: Ready: none / "no ready issues. Next: /plan".
After:  Ready: 194 more / "forge claim forge-2agy.2, then /plan".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
